ABBA verdict 2026-09-14: REFUTED. This commit's premise (collapsing 18
narrow Q8_0 weight loads to 3 wide ones relieves issue-slot/MIO pressure) does not hold
end-to-end. ABBA n=6 under one host lease, arms verified by grepping the built tree for
DS4_Q80_STAGE_UINT4: A(baseline) mean 12.340 t/s, B(staged) mean 12.360 t/s = +0.16%,
inside the 0.73% all-draw spread. A tie.

The SASS prediction was met exactly -- per-loop census, base 140 ins LDG=27 LDS=0
{LDG.E.U16:16, LDG.E.U8:2}; staged 144 ins LDG=12 LDS=17 {LDG.E.128:3, STS.128:3,
LDS.U16:17}. But total weight memory instructions ROSE 18 -> 23 and the loop gained two
__syncwarp() per iteration: the staging copies the 34-byte interleave into shared memory
instead of repacking it. Load count fell; instruction count did not.

Caveats on the measurement: both cudaprof captures failed rc=2 (capture journal exceeded
limit of 1048576 records; this workload needs --max-records=8000000), so the per-kernel
device time of ds4_q8_0_matvec_quantized was never obtained -- only the end-to-end bound
that it moved less than ~10%. The bit-identity gate printed one arm's prefill sha256 with
no counterpart to compare against, so it gated nothing.

Two claims in this commit's message and in cuda_q80_layout.cu:6-8 are WITHDRAWN by their
author: (1) the 34-byte stride does not 'spread each load across the whole run' -- 1088
bytes requires 34 sectors as an arithmetic floor, so there is no amplification; (2) the
alignment holds for ODD b, not even (34b+2 = 2(17b+1)).

Do not merge this branch as an optimization. Its value is the null result.
